  "lineage": "Late-1920s jazz style developed in Chicago by young white musicians influenced by the New Orleans tradition transplanted to Chicago through the 1917 closure of Storyville. Characterized by greater solo-improvisation emphasis (vs Dixieland collective-improvisation), introduction of tenor saxophone, four-beat-to-the-bar rhythm (vs Dixieland's two-beat), and harder-edged \"Chicago bite\" articulation. The Austin High Gang (Bud Freeman, Frank Teschemacher, Jimmy McPartland) is the canonical Chicago school.",
